const body = `<h1>8.6a — Dungeon Maps From Files</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Unit 8 gave you the grid. Unit 7.6 gave you files. Today they merge into the
        most satisfying structure in the whole course: a dungeon map that loads from a
        text file, where every wall, floor, monster, and the player position is just a
        character in a text file you can edit. Level design becomes text editing.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Download the maps first.</strong>
        <a href="../data/dungeon1.txt" download>dungeon1.txt</a> (small tutorial map)
        and <a href="../data/dungeon2.txt" download>dungeon2.txt</a> (the real
        dungeon). Legend: <code>#</code> wall, <code>.</code> floor,
        <code>@</code> player start, <code>G</code> goblin, spaces outside the walls
        mean nothing. Open both in tabs and read them with your eyes first. Can you
        find the goblin? Can you see the path from @ to G?
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Part 1: Load the Map</h3>
        <p>Create <code>DungeonLoader.java</code>. The pattern is a direct upgrade of
        7.6a: each line of the file is a ROW of the grid.</p>
        <pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;
import java.io.File;

Scanner in = new Scanner(new File("dungeon1.txt"));

// Pass 1: count the rows
int rows = 0;
while (in.hasNext()) { in.nextLine(); rows++; }
in.close();

// Reopen and fill
in = new Scanner(new File("dungeon1.txt"));
char[][] map = new char[rows][];
int r = 0;
while (in.hasNext()) {
    map[r] = in.nextLine().toCharArray();
    r++;
}
in.close();</code></pre>
        <p>Run it and print the map with nested loops to prove it loaded. Then answer:
        how many columns does row 0 have? Does every row have the same length?
        (Check with code: <code>map[r].length</code>. If they differ, your bounds
        checking must use <code>map[r].length</code> per row, not one fixed width.
        This is the jagged-array trap, and the CED tests it.)</p>
        <p><strong>Why two passes?</strong> You need the row count before you can
        create the array. The reopen trick (close, new Scanner) is the simple
        solution. An ArrayList of char[] would skip the counting pass entirely. Build
        it once each way; feel the difference.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Part 2: Find the Features</h3>
        <p>Write <code>find(char target)</code> that scans the grid and returns the
        row and column of the first match (print them, or return an int[] {row, col}).
        Find <code>@</code>. Find <code>G</code>. Then write
        <code>count(char target)</code>: how many walls? How many floor tiles? And a
        <code>printLegendCounts()</code> that prints a little census of every
        character in the map. Your 8.2 traversal patterns, now with meaning.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Part 3: Level Design Is Text Editing</h3>
        <p>Copy dungeon1.txt to dungeon3.txt in your folder. Redesign it: move the
        goblin, extend a corridor, add a second goblin (<code>g</code>), add a
        treasure room with <code>$</code>. Do not touch your Java. Run the loader on
        dungeon3.txt. It just works. That feeling, of the world changing because you
        edited text, is the entire point of data-driven design. Now load dungeon2.txt
        and count the goblins: is one hiding in a pocket you did not notice?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The Map That Ate Itself</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Remove the <code>in.close()</code> between the two passes and reopen
            without a fresh Scanner. What happens? Trace any error back to its
            cause — how far is the error from the thing that caused it?</li>
            <li>Trailing whitespace: add two spaces after the last wall on some row.
            Now row lengths differ by 2. Which of your loops, if any, breaks?</li>
            <li>Empty line at the end of the file: some editors add one. Does your
            code survive it? If not, guard it.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is data-driven level design better than generating maps in code?
            Give the designer argument and the modder argument, then check your
            answer against the 7.6a Socratic. Same argument, bigger stakes.</li>
            <li>Your map loader uses nextLine + toCharArray. The JQR also offers
            <code>next()</code>. What would the map look like if you read it with
            next() instead of nextLine()? Try it on dungeon1.txt and see what breaks.
            (Spaces are delimiters to next(). Maps are made of spaces. Catastrophe.)</li>
            <li>Exam connection: FRQ 4 gives you a 2D array and asks you to traverse
            and process it. Your census, find, and count methods ARE FRQ 4 methods.
            Which one maps to "count occurrences in a grid"? Which to "locate a
            target"?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Load a text-based map into a char[][] with the two-pass pattern</li>
            <li>Handle jagged rows with per-row bounds checking</li>
            <li>Write find/count/census methods over a loaded grid</li>
            <li>Explain data-driven design and defend it</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/maps-from-files.html">Dungeon Maps From Files</a> — the full pattern guide</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/reading-files.html">Reading Files</a> — the Scanner side</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-basics.html">2D Array Basics</a> — the grid side</li>
        <li><a href="../data/dungeon1.txt">dungeon1.txt</a> · <a href="../data/dungeon2.txt">dungeon2.txt</a> — the maps</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
